1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
This is Task 12, Jenkins and Docker continuous deployment.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Continuous deployment means that every change that passes the tests is delivered automatically.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Here is the proof from the build I started live in the previous video.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
The command reads the admin password from the environment file without showing it.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:28,000
The latest build is number twelve, named with the version and the commit, and the result is success.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:36,000
The stages show the quality gate: the tests come first, and the images are built, published and deployed only after them.

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,000
How is the release identified?

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
The tag of every image is the version from the VERSION file, a dash, and the short commit id.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Here are the three pieces: the version, the commit at the tip of develop, and the tag I compute from them, which is exactly the tag of the build.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,000
The Jenkinsfile builds it in the checkout stage.

11
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
So the tag tells you which release and which commit an image contains.

12
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Now the Jenkins log.

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Docker build creates the application image with the registry prefix and the tag, and passes the version and the commit as build arguments.

14
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Then docker push publishes it to the local registry, and the registry answers with a digest, a unique fingerprint of the image.

15
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,000
The same happens for the Nginx image.

16
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
The registry itself.

17
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:48,000
I ask it directly, over its HTTP interface, which repositories it holds: the application and Nginx.

18
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:56,000
And which tags the application has: every release that the pipeline ever published, version and commit.

19
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
The new one is among them.

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:05,000
These older tags are also the candidates for a rollback, because nothing has to be rebuilt.

21
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
The image carries its own identity.

22
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
I inspect the image from the registry tag and print its labels.

23
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
The revision label holds the commit id, and the version label the version.

24
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,000
These labels are written at build time from the build arguments.

25
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
So even without Jenkins, anyone can ask an image which commit it was built from.

26
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Deployment.

27
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Jenkins runs docker compose pull, which fetches the images that were just published, then docker compose up with the wait option.

28
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Look at the log: the staging app container was recreated, started, and Docker reported it healthy, then the Nginx container as well.

29
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
It was recreated because the new image has a new identity.

30
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
The database stays safe, because it lives in a separate volume.

31
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Is the container running the image that was tested and published?

32
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,000
I compare two image ids.

33
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:20,000
The first is the image the running staging container was created from.

34
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,000
The second is the image behind the tag in the registry.

35
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,000
They are identical, character for character.

36
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
The tested image became the deployed release, with no rebuild in between.

37
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,000
The container list shows both staging containers, healthy.

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Finally the health verification.

39
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:52,000
I ask the running staging application for its health: status ok, environment staging, the version, and the commit.

40
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:01,000
I compare the commit with the tip of develop, and with the health file that the pipeline itself saved during its health check stage.

41
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:02,000
All the same.

42
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,000
The deployed version is traceable to one commit and one Jenkins build.

43
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
I must be honest about Docker Hub.

44
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:26,000
The pipeline supports it: if the registry parameter does not start with localhost, it logs in with a Jenkins credential called registry creds, and the password is masked in the log.

45
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
These are the lines.

46
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:33,000
But I never ran it, because it needs a Docker Hub account and an access token.

47
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Everything shown in this video used the local registry.

48
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:45,000
To publish externally I would set the registry parameter to docker dot io slash my id and add the credential in Jenkins.

49
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,000
I will not publish anything or store credentials without your authorization.


